# =====================================================================
# WHAT BELONGS IN THIS REPO
# =====================================================================
# Code, and the one data file the deployed dashboard cannot run without.
# Everything else in cache/ is REGENERABLE by re-running the scripts, and
# putting it in git costs a lot for nothing:
#
#   .git was 1.1 GB against 1.8 GB of cache, with 294 Statcast pulls and
#   2,870 cache files tracked. GitHub soft-warns at 1 GB and refuses
#   pushes over 2 GB, and every night's run adds more.
#
# The deployed Streamlit app reads exactly ONE thing: cache/slate_*.csv.
# dashboard.py imports only glob, os, pandas, streamlit and altair -- no
# project modules -- so nothing else in cache/ is reachable from it.
# predict_slate.py needs the rest, and predict_slate.py runs on your
# machine, not on Streamlit's.
# =====================================================================

# --- Everything in cache/, then the exceptions -----------------------
# NOTE the pattern is `cache/*` and not `cache/`. Git will not look inside
# an ignored DIRECTORY, so ignoring `cache/` would make the negations
# below unreachable and the slate files would never be committed.
cache/*
!cache/slate_*.csv

# The Results tab reads this. It is NOT regenerable the way the rest of
# cache/ is: rescoring an old date needs that date's slate file AND the
# Statcast pull behind it, and the whole point of the running record is
# that it accumulates one honest out-of-sample row per prop per day. It
# grows about seven rows a night -- a few hundred KB a season. Leaving it
# untracked is why the deployed dashboard said "No slates scored yet"
# while the local file had 2026-08-18 in it all along.
!cache/scoring_log.csv

# THE RULE THIS KEEPS RE-LEARNING: anything the dashboard READS, or any
# record that ACCUMULATES, has to be tracked. Everything else in cache/ is
# regenerable; these are not.
#
# It has now bitten twice. The Results tab said "No slates scored yet" for
# a day while scoring_log.csv sat populated on disk, and the Pitchers tab
# was about to do the same thing. In both cases the code was correct, the
# data existed, and the deployed app was telling the truth about what it
# could see.

# Read by the Pitchers tab. One row per starter per slate, ~5 KB a night.
!cache/pitchers_*.csv

# Accumulating records. Neither is regenerable: rescoring an old date needs
# that date's prediction file AND the Statcast pull behind it, and the
# whole value is that they add one honest out-of-sample row per night.
#   form_log.csv            does the hot/cold marker predict anything
#   pitcher_scoring_log.csv how the strikeout model is actually doing
!cache/form_log.csv
!cache/pitcher_scoring_log.csv

# Regenerable, and the expensive part of the repo:
#   statcast_player_*.csv    ~1.2 GB, re-pulled by predict_slate.py
#   statcast_pitcher_*.csv   re-pulled per slate, ~30 starters at a time
#   batting_lines.csv        ~10 MB, one ~30-min backfill
#   lineup_slots.csv         ~6 MB, refetched incrementally
#   game_context.csv         weather/venue, refetched incrementally
# Losing these costs time, not information -- but do NOT delete them
# casually. Untracking (see below) leaves them on disk untouched.

# --- Python ----------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.Python

# --- Virtual environments --------------------------------------------
# The venv lives at C:\venvs\baseball, OUTSIDE this folder, deliberately:
# OneDrive locks files inside synced directories and corrupted the first
# one mid-delete. venv_broken/ is the corpse of that attempt.
venv/
venv_broken/
.venv/
env/
ENV/

# --- Editors / OS ----------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------
# The Odds API key. Git history is forever and this repo is public, so a
# key committed once has to be rotated, not deleted. Prefer the
# ODDS_API_KEY environment variable; this file is the simpler fallback.
.odds_api_key

# --- Streamlit -------------------------------------------------------
# secrets.toml can hold credentials. config.toml is fine to commit if you
# want the deployed theme to match local.
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# --- Scratch ---------------------------------------------------------
*.log
chart_check*.png
/tmp/
